BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260517
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260518
LOCATION:Menucha Retreat and Conference Center\, 38711 E\, Historic Colum
 bia River Hwy\, Corbett\, OR
SUMMARY:Liberatory Leadership: Embracing Grief and Possibility While Buil
 ding Beloved Community (In-Person)
DESCRIPTION:This Courage to Lead® retreat is designed to help participan
 ts renew their passion\, enthusiasm\, and commitment to themselves and th
 e communities they serve\, and is open to those identifying as women who 
 are in leadership—whether formally or informally.  This four-day offeri
 ng is for those seeking to hold and respond to the complexity of our time
 \, while finding rest and rejuvenation through practicing community toget
 her. We will meet during the fullness of spring when the abundance of lig
 ht and energy invites us to remember all that brought us to this moment. 
  As individuals and as leaders\, we can expand our capacity to be fully p
 resent to ourselves and our community\, and reclaim our power to meet our
  calling in these increasingly challenging times. With courage\, compassi
 on and creativity we will share practices to hold our current realities\,
  wherever we find ourselves.We will invite the beauty of this special lan
 d above the Columbia Gorge to be a teacher and guide.Together we will…P
 ause\, breathe\, embrace silence\, and disconnect from technology and our
  demanding pace of life  Reduce isolation and build communities of care t
 hat provide support in hard times\, and act as containers for liberatory 
 innovations  Identify reflective practices that help us embrace grief and
  build hope for the future of our communities  Deepen our skills to ask o
 pen-ended questions that help us and other to uncover inner wisdom  Expan
 d capacity to be fully present to ourselves and others in ways that affir
 m and heal  Strengthen our ability to hold paradox\, moving beyond either
 -or thinking into holding opposing truths and complexity  Learn and parti
 cipate in a Clearness Committee (https://couragerenewal.org/library/the-c
 learness-committee-a-communal-approach-to-discernment/)\, designed after 
 a powerful problem-solving model from the 1600's    We are excited to hav
 e you join in retreat\, grounded in the Courage &amp\; Renewal® approach
 \, upon which Circles of Trust (http://couragerenewal.org/approach/)® is
  based\, designed to build a trustworthy space for telling our truths and
  listening to the truths of others.Retreat Details &amp\;&#160\;Registrat
 ionDate and Time: Sunday-Wednesday\, May 17-20\, 2026. The retreat will b
 egin at 4 p.m. Sunday and conclude at 11 a.m. on Wednesday.Location: Menu
 cha Retreat and Conference Center\, Corbett\, ORFacilitators: Anita Garci
 a Morales\; Dawn Montgomery\, EdD\; and Jen Wagley\, MEd\, MDivCost: Regi
 stration fees include three nights of lodging\, 9 meals (beginning Sunday
  dinner and ending Wednesday Breakfast)\, and all supplies\, snacks\, bev
 erages\, and 18 PDUs or CEUs.Single Occupancy Room: $1\,195  Shared Occup
 ancy: $895  Register Online (https://lclark.tfaforms.net/4735427?epid=a1a
 UY0000023cE5)Accessibility:We are committed to making our events accessib
 le to all needs and abilities. When registering\, please let us know your
  access needs. Contact us at 503-768-6040 or cce@lclark.edu with question
 s.Scholarship Fund: We strive to keep our Courage retreats accessible to 
 all who wish to participate. Please consider making a tax-deductible dona
 tion (https://engage.lclark.edu/register/gsecgiving?sys:gift:fund=4dfb9f4
 e-7351-4d84-91fe-531631c248c7&amp\;sys:gift:notes=Courage%20and%20Renewal
 %20Retreats%20Scholarship%20Fund) to our scholarship fund.About the Facil
 itatorsAnita Garcia Morales retired from Seattle Public Schools after 35 
 years of teaching and learning from students\, coaching/mentoring educato
 rs\, and designing and facilitating DEIB sessions for all district employ
 ees. Through 'Racing to Equity'\, a consulting group Anita co-founded and
  co-owns\, she facilitates anti-bias circles with community groups\, gove
 rnment agencies\, students\, educators and works with immigrant families 
 who are just learning to navigate the complexities of their newly adopted
  home. Anita is a Courage &amp\; Renewal Facilitator\, a Positive Discipl
 ine Certified Associate\, and a Class Action Certified Trainer. The commo
 n thread that runs through all that Anita does is her focus on social jus
 tice and equity. Anita is on a journey of self-healing from the generatio
 nal trauma that she\, like IBPOC across the country\, have inherited and 
 continue to experience. Anita also sees herself as a healer calling up he
 r ancestral gifts and gathers circles of folx on similar paths of healing
  and liberation. In this work\, IBPOC center themselves in their quest to
  show up in the world more authentically and unapologetically.Dawn Montgo
 mery\, EdD is a change facilitator and insightful thought partner. She su
 pports leaders from a wide variety of fields\, including educators\, nonp
 rofit executives\, and community administrators as they find their way in
  challenging times. Assisting humans in their discovery and reconnection 
 to their deepest longings has been a passion for many years. Her backgrou
 nd in K-12 and college education as teacher\, principal\, and district le
 ader support her coaching practice and team facilitation. She believes tr
 ansformation is born when a circle of humans sit together to listen\, to 
 speak their deepest truths and to share their wildest imaginations for th
 e world. She is expert at holding space for both individual leaders and t
 eams as an executive coach\, conflict mediator\, equity-focused professio
 nal developer and a certified Courage &amp\; Renewal facilitator. She is 
 committed to making a difference in the lives of individuals and their co
 mmunities.Jen Wagley\, MEd\, M Div serves as Deputy Director of Houston i
 n Action. She formerly served as the founding Executive Director of Our C
 hildren Oregon\, an advocacy and research organization committed to impro
 ving child well-being. She brings two decades of experience in organizati
 onal leadership\, grass-roots power building and comprehensive community 
 development and engagement. Jenifer is a facilitator with the Center for 
 Courage &amp\; Renewal. Her work with the Center has been key to her sust
 ainability as a leader and has provided her the opportunity of supporting
  other leaders across the country. Previously she served as the Deputy Di
 rector of Avenue Community Development Corporation in Houston\, TX. Jenif
 er is driven by a passion to create a more just\, healthy\, loving societ
 y where every person/being has what they need to thrive. Jen is mom to th
 ree boys and loves to hike\, coach basketball and build friendships. Jeni
 fer holds two master's degrees from Baylor University and Truett Seminary
 .&#160\;New workshops and trainings are added to our calendar regularly. 
 For the latest on professional development related to your specific inter
 ests\, sign up for our mailing list! (https://lclark.tfaforms.net/4735441
 )
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  This Courage
  to Lead® retreat is designed to help participants renew their passion\,
  enthusiasm\, and commitment to themselves and the communities they serve
 \, and is open to those identifying as women who are in leadership—whet
 her formally or informally.</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  This four-
 day offering is for those seeking to hold and respond to the complexity o
 f our time\, while finding rest and rejuvenation through practicing commu
 nity together. We will meet during the fullness of spring when the abunda
 nce of light and energy invites us to remember all that brought us to thi
 s moment.</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  As individuals and as leader
 s\, we can expand our capacity to be fully present to ourselves and our c
 ommunity\, and reclaim our power to meet our calling in these increasingl
 y challenging times. <span class="s2">With courage\, compassion and creat
 ivity we will share practices to hold our current realities\, wherever we
  find ourselves.</span></p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><span class="s1"
 >We will invite the beauty of this special land above the Columbia Gorge 
 to be a teacher and guide.</span></p><p><strong>Together we will…</stro
 ng></p><ul><li style="line-height: 1.5\;">Pause\, breathe\, embrace silen
 ce\, and disconnect from technology and our demanding pace of life  </li>
 <li style="line-height: 1.5\;">Reduce isolation and build communities of 
 care that provide support in hard times\, and act as containers for liber
 atory innovations  </li><li style="line-height: 1.5\;">Identify reflectiv
 e practices that help us embrace grief and build hope for the future of o
 ur communities  </li><li style="line-height: 1.5\;">Deepen our skills to 
 ask open-ended questions that help us and other to uncover inner wisdom  
 </li><li style="line-height: 1.5\;">Expand capacity to be fully present t
 o ourselves and others in ways that affirm and heal  </li><li style="line
 -height: 1.5\;">Strengthen our ability to hold paradox\, moving beyond ei
 ther-or thinking into holding opposing truths and complexity  </li><li st
 yle="line-height: 1.5\;">Learn and participate in a <a href="https://cour
 agerenewal.org/library/the-clearness-committee-a-communal-approach-to-dis
 cernment/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Clearness Committee</a>\, desig
 ned after a powerful problem-solving model from the 1600's  </li></ul><p 
 style="line-height: 1.5\;">  We are excited to have you join in retreat\,
  grounded in the Courage &amp\; Renewal® approach\, upon which <a href="
 http://couragerenewal.org/approach/">Circles of Trust</a>® is based\, de
 signed to build a trustworthy space for telling our truths and listening 
 to the truths of others.</p><hr /><h2><strong>Retreat Details &amp\;&#160
 \;</strong><strong>Registration</strong></h2><p style="line-height: 1.5\;
 "><strong>Date and Time:</strong> Sunday-Wednesday\, May 17-20\, 2026. Th
 e retreat will begin at 4 p.m. Sunday and conclude at 11 a.m. on Wednesda
 y.</p><p><strong>Location:</strong> Menucha Retreat and Conference Center
 \, Corbett\, OR</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Facilitators:</s
 trong> Anita Garcia Morales\; Dawn Montgomery\, EdD\; and Jen Wagley\, ME
 d\, MDiv</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Cost:</strong> Registra
 tion fees include three nights of lodging\, 9 meals (beginning Sunday din
 ner and ending Wednesday Breakfast)\, and all supplies\, snacks\, beverag
 es\, and 18 PDUs or CEUs.</p><ul><li><strong>Single Occupancy Room:</stro
 ng> $1\,195  </li><li><strong>Shared Occupancy:</strong> $895  </li></ul>
 <p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong style="background-color: transparen
 t\;"><a class="btn-small-style-1" href="https://lclark.tfaforms.net/47354
 27?epid=a1aUY0000023cE5" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Register Online</
 a></strong></p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong style="background-co
 lor: transparent\;">Accessibility:</strong><span style="background-color:
  transparent\;">We are committed to making our events accessible to all n
 eeds and abilities. When registering\, please let us know your access nee
 ds. Contact us at 503-768-6040 or cce@lclark.edu with questions</span><sp
 an style="font-weight: 400\;">.</span></p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><
 strong>Scholarship Fund:</strong> We strive to keep our Courage retreats 
 accessible to all who wish to participate. Please consider making a <a hr
 ef="https://engage.lclark.edu/register/gsecgiving?sys:gift:fund=4dfb9f4e-
 7351-4d84-91fe-531631c248c7&amp\;sys:gift:notes=Courage%20and%20Renewal%2
 0Retreats%20Scholarship%20Fund">tax-deductible donation</a> to our schola
 rship fund.</p><hr /><h2><strong>About the Facilitators</strong></h2><p><
 picture class="lw_image lw_image107036 lw_align_right"><source type="imag
 e/png" srcset="https://www.lclark.edu/live/image/gid/91/width/170/height/
 170/107036_Anita_Garcia_Morales.rev.1738088338.png 1x" data-origin="respo
 nsive"/><picture>
	<source type="image/png" srcset="https://www.lclark.edu/live/image/gid/91/width/170/height/170  /107036_Anita_Garcia_Morales.rev.1738088338.png 1x" data-origin="responsive"/>
	<img width="170" height="170" alt="" data-alt="" data-decoration="true" src="https://www.lclark.edu/live/image/gid/91/width/170/height/170  /107036_Anita_Garcia_Morales.rev.1738088338.png" title="Anita Garcia Mora  les" data-max-w="287" data-max-h="287" loading="lazy" data-optimized="true"/>
</picture>
</picture></p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Anita Garcia Mora
 les</strong> retired from Seattle Public Schools after 35 years of teachi
 ng and learning from students\, coaching/mentoring educators\, and design
 ing and facilitating DEIB sessions for all district employees. Through 'R
 acing to Equity'\, a consulting group Anita co-founded and co-owns\, she 
 facilitates anti-bias circles with community groups\, government agencies
 \, students\, educators and works with immigrant families who are just le
 arning to navigate the complexities of their newly adopted home. Anita is
  a Courage &amp\; Renewal Facilitator\, a Positive Discipline Certified A
 ssociate\, and a Class Action Certified Trainer. The common thread that r
 uns through all that Anita does is her focus on social justice and equity
 . Anita is on a journey of self-healing from the generational trauma that
  she\, like IBPOC across the country\, have inherited and continue to exp
 erience. Anita also sees herself as a healer calling up her ancestral gif
 ts and gathers circles of folx on similar paths of healing and liberation
 . In this work\, IBPOC center themselves in their quest to show up in the
  world more authentically and unapologetically.</p><p><picture class="lw_
