BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261002
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261003
LOCATION:Lewis &amp\; Clark Graduate School
SUMMARY:Writing into Presence: Reflective Practices for Courageous Leader
 ship (In-Person)
DESCRIPTION:Where do you feel called to create new possibilities in your 
 life\, community\, or leadership?What role does reflective practice have 
 in your leadership now?Is there a writing\, creative or nature-based prac
 tice that you'd like to nurture with intention to support your life and l
 eadership?How do we make space for our inner experience while staying con
 nected to creativity and hope during uncertain and challenging times?  Tr
 ue leadership is an act of integrity—a bridge between our deepest perso
 nal values and the public work of social change. Wherever your work may b
 e\, in a classroom\, the therapy room\, a grassroots movement\, a family\
 , an organization\, leadership for social change begins with the courage 
 to lead from the heart.  Reflective practices nurture our capacity to lea
 d courageously from the integrity of our whole selves. Join us in this Co
 urage &amp\; Renewal® weekend immersion where we will explore writing in
  multiple forms as well as other creative expressions to support connecti
 on with the heart and soul that sustains our capacity to live and lead wi
 th presence\, creativity\, and hope.    Bless the notebook that I always 
 carry in my pocket.    And the pen.    Bless the words with which I try t
 o saw what I see\, think\, or feel.    With gratitude for the grace of th
 e earth.    The expected and the expectation\, both.    For the house I h
 ave been given to be in this world.      from Good Morning by Mary Oliver
     During this retreat\, ample time will be offered for writing\, includ
 ing wandering on the beautiful campus grounds at Lewis &amp\; Clark. Writ
 ing will be approached as a personal contemplative practice\, not a craft
  or skill to be mastered or critiqued.Together we will explore reflective
  practice for courageous leadership through:      Writing and reflecting 
 in nature          Exploring the skills of Presencing and strategies of H
 ope          Expanding our capacity to be present with what is\, and lear
 n skills for responding in life-giving ways          Engaging curiosity a
 nd open honest questions as a way to deepen our writing practice and gain
  clarity around issues in our lives    Details &amp\; RegistrationDates a
 nd Times: Friday-Sunday\, October 2-4\, 2026Friday: 9:30 a.m.-5 p.m.Satur
 day: 9:30 a.m.-5 p.m.Sunday: 9:30 a.m.-12:30 p.m.Location: Lewis &amp\; C
 lark Graduate School\, 615 S. Palatine Hill Road\, Portland\, ORFacilitat
 ors: Dawn Montgomery\, EdD\; Matsya SiosalRegistration: $425. Includes 16
  PDUs or CEUs. Registration fees include Friday and Saturday lunch\, and 
 all supplies\, snacks and beverages.Register Online (https://lclark.tfafo
 rms.net/4735427?epid=a1aUY000002h2zt)Lodging: Please note that this is no
 t a residential retreat. The Lakeshore Inn (https://www.thelakeshoreinn.c
 om/) is the closest lodging option to campus\, and other options with rat
 es for L&amp\;C visitors can be found here (https://www.lclark.edu/visit/
 places_to_stay/).Accessibility: We are committed to making our events acc
 essible to all needs and abilities. When registering\, please let us know
  your access needs. Contact us at 503-768-6040 or cce@lclark.edu with que
 stions.Scholarship Fund: We strive to keep our Courage retreats accessibl
 e to all who wish to participate. Please consider making a tax-deductible
  donation (https://engage.lclark.edu/register/gsecgiving?sys:gift:fund=4d
 fb9f4e-7351-4d84-91fe-531631c248c7&amp\;sys:gift:notes=Courage%20and%20Re
 newal%20Retreats%20Scholarship%20Fund) to our scholarship fund.About the 
 Facilitators Dawn Montgomery\, EdD is a change facilitator and insightful
  thought partner. She supports leaders from a wide variety of fields\, in
 cluding educators\, nonprofit executives\, and community administrators a
 s they find their way in challenging times. Assisting humans in their dis
 covery and reconnection to their deepest longings has been a passion for 
 many years. Her background in K-12 and college education as teacher\, pri
 ncipal\, and district leader support her coaching practice and team facil
 itation. She believes transformation is born when a circle of humans sit 
 together to listen\, to speak their deepest truths and to share their wil
 dest imaginations for the world. She is expert at holding space for both 
 individual leaders and teams as an executive coach\, conflict mediator\, 
 equity-focused professional developer and a certified Courage &amp\; Rene
 wal facilitator. She is committed to making a difference in the lives of 
 individuals and their communities. Matsya Siosal has developed her skills
  in leadership and facilitation for over 25 years\, including roles with 
 small and large for-profit corporations\, mission-driven consumer goods c
 ompanies\, higher education\, and as a consultant and entrepreneur. She h
 as served as the director of the Center for Community Engagement at Lewis
  &amp\; Clark since 2015\, after joining the department in 2010 to work i
 n marketing and program management. Matsya is also co-founder and managin
 g partner of Sea-O-Salt Bath Company (http://www.seaosalt.com/). Matsya i
 s an experienced certified facilitator of Courage &amp\; Renewal programs
 . She has facilitated Circle of Trust\, Courage to Lead\, and Leading Tog
 ether workshops and retreats since 2019\, and is trained as a SoulCollage
 ® (https://soulcollage.com/) facilitator. As a facilitator and coach\, M
 atsya supports mission-driven leaders in accessing their authentic voice\
 , and finding clarity and self-trust so they can show up with wholehearte
 dness and sustainability in their lives and work.New workshops and traini
 ngs are added to our calendar regularly. For the latest on professional d
 evelopment related to your specific interests\, sign up for our mailing l
 ist! (https://lclark.tfaforms.net/4735441)
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><em>Where do y
 ou feel called to create new possibilities in your life\, community\, or 
 leadership?</em></p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><em>What role does refl
 ective practice have in your leadership now?</em></p><p style="line-heigh
 t: 1.5\;"><em>Is there a writing\, creative or nature-based practice that
  you'd like to nurture with intention to support your life and leadership
 ?</em></p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><em>How do we make space for our 
 inner experience while staying connected to creativity and hope during un
 certain and challenging times?</em></p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  Tr
 ue leadership is an act of integrity—a bridge between our deepest perso
 nal values and the public work of social change. Wherever your work may b
 e\, in a classroom\, the therapy room\, a grassroots movement\, a family\
 , an organization\, leadership for social change begins with the courage 
 to lead from the heart.</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  Reflective pra
 ctices nurture our capacity to lead courageously from the integrity of ou
 r whole selves. Join us in this Courage &amp\; Renewal® weekend immersio
 n where we will explore writing in multiple forms as well as other creati
 ve expressions to support connection with the heart and soul that sustain
 s our capacity to live and lead with presence\, creativity\, and hope.</p
 ><blockquote><p style="font-size: 2.2rem\;">    Bless the notebook that I
  always carry in my pocket.<br />    And the pen.<br />    Bless the word
 s with which I try to saw what I see\, think\, or feel.<br />    With gra
 titude for the grace of the earth.<br />    The expected and the expectat
 ion\, both.<br />    For the house I have been given to be in this world.
   </p><p class="quote-author" style="text-align: right\;">    from <em>Go
 od Morning</em> by Mary Oliver  </p></blockquote><p style="line-height: 1
 .5\;">  During this retreat\, ample time will be offered for writing\, in
 cluding wandering on the beautiful campus grounds at Lewis &amp\; Clark. 
 Writing will be approached as a personal contemplative practice\, not a c
 raft or skill to be mastered or critiqued.</p><h4 style="line-height: 1.5
 \;"><strong>Together we will explore reflective practice for courageous l
 eadership through:<br /></strong></h4><ul><li><p style="line-height: 1.5\
 ;">      Writing and reflecting in nature    </p></li><li><p style="line-
 height: 1.5\;">      Exploring the skills of Presencing and strategies of
  Hope    </p></li><li><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">      Expanding our c
 apacity to be present with what is\, and learn skills for responding in l
 ife-giving ways    </p></li><li><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">      Engag
 ing curiosity and open honest questions as a way to deepen our writing pr
 actice and gain clarity around issues in our lives    </p></li></ul><hr /
 ><h2><strong>Details &amp\; Registration</strong></h2><p style="line-heig
 ht: 1.5\;"><strong>Dates and Times:</strong> Friday-Sunday\, October 2-4\
 , 2026</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\; padding-left: 40px\;"><strong>Frid
 ay:</strong> 9:30 a.m.-5 p.m.<br /><strong>Saturday:</strong> 9:30 a.m.-5
  p.m.<br /><strong>Sunday:</strong> 9:30 a.m.-12:30 p.m.</p><p style="lin
 e-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Location:</strong> Lewis &amp\; Clark Graduate S
 chool\, 615 S. Palatine Hill Road\, Portland\, OR</p><p style="line-heigh
 t: 1.5\;"><strong>Facilitators:</strong> Dawn Montgomery\, EdD\; Matsya S
 iosal</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Registration:</strong> $42
 5. Includes 16 PDUs or CEUs. Registration fees include Friday and Saturda
 y lunch\, and all supplies\, snacks and beverages.</p><p style="line-heig
 ht: 1.5\;"><strong><a class="btn-small-style-1" href="https://lclark.tfaf
 orms.net/4735427?epid=a1aUY000002h2zt">Register Online</a></strong></p><p
  style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Lodging:</strong> Please note that th
 is <span style="text-decoration: underline\;"><strong>is not</strong></sp
 an> a residential retreat. <a href="https://www.thelakeshoreinn.com/">The
  Lakeshore Inn</a> is the closest lodging option to campus\, and other op
 tions with rates for L&amp\;C visitors can be <a href="https://www.lclark
 .edu/visit/places_to_stay/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">found here</a>
 .</p><p class="p1" style="font-size: 14px\; line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>A
 ccessibility:</strong> We are committed to making our events accessible t
 o all needs and abilities. When registering\, please let us know your acc
 ess needs. Contact us at 503-768-6040 or cce@lclark.edu with questions.</
 p><p class="p1" style="font-size: 14px\; line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Scho
 larship Fund:</strong> We strive to keep our Courage retreats accessible 
 to all who wish to participate. Please consider making a <a href="https:/
 /engage.lclark.edu/register/gsecgiving?sys:gift:fund=4dfb9f4e-7351-4d84-9
 1fe-531631c248c7&amp\;sys:gift:notes=Courage%20and%20Renewal%20Retreats%2
 0Scholarship%20Fund">tax-deductible donation</a> to our scholarship fund.
 </p><hr /><h2 style="line-height: 1.6\;"><strong>About the Facilitators</
 strong></h2><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong><picture class="lw_imag
